WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.490
Let us consider a system with an ideal gas
in it.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:10.760
The state of the gas, or more precisely, its
temperature T, its pressure Pi and its volume

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:20.550
V are described by the ideal gas equation:
Pi is equal to n times R times T times 1 divided

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:26.960
by V. &nbsp;n is the amount of substance and indirectly
describes the number of gas particles and

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.929
R is the gas constant.

00:00:30.929 --> 00:00:36.540
We refer to temperature, pressure and volume
as properties - they describe the macroscopic

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.010
state of the gas.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:47.280
If we heat the gas and thus increase its temperature
T, we are talking about a thermodynamic process.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.370
We could also compress the gas, thereby increasing
the pressure and reducing the volume - this

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.270
would also be a thermodynamic process.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:04.729
The thermodynamic process can be isobaric,
isothermal, isochoric or adiabatic.

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:11.330
In the case of an isobaric process, the pressure
of the gas remains constant.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:18.479
This means that Pi, n and R are pure constants
in the gas law and the volume V is proportional

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:19.869
to the temperature T.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:29.120
The pressure-volume diagram results in a horizontal
straight line for an isobaric gas.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:36.560
This means that a change in volume does NOT
lead to a change in gas pressure.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.729
The volume V of the gas remains constant during
an isochoric process.

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:45.710
Then V, n and R are constants in the gas law,
and the pressure Pi is proportional to the

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.670
temperature T.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.520
The pressure-volume diagram shows a vertical
straight line for an isochoric gas.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.680
The change in gas pressure does NOT lead to
a change in volume.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:05.890
In an isothermal process, the temperature
T of the gas remains constant.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:13.590
This means that the pressure Pi is proportional
to the inverse volume: 1 by V.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:20.750
The pressure-volume diagram shows a decreasing
curve for an isothermally behaving gas.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:25.530
Increasing the volume of the gas leads to
a reduction in the gas pressure.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:31.900
During an adiabatic process, no thermal energy
is transported OUT and into the system.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.379
During this thermodynamic process, the temperature
T, volume V and pressure Pi of the gas can

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:39.710
change simultaneously.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:46.550
The pressure-volume diagram shows a power
law for an adiabatically behaving gas.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.620
The exponent gamma is referred to as the adiabatic
exponent.

